/** @page enums Enums: Type-Safe Enumerated Constants

    @tableofcontents

    @section enum_overview Enum Overview
    The <tt><b>enum</b></tt> keyword allows for type-safe enumerated constants to be declared with a
    specific base type. Enums provide named constants that are grouped together and can be used for
    type-safe programming where only specific values are valid.

    An enum value converts implicitly to the enum's base type, so enum constants can be used wherever the
    base type is expected.  The reverse conversion is not implicit: a value of the base type cannot be
    assigned to an enum-typed variable or passed to an enum-typed parameter, even when it equals the value
    of a member; it must be converted with @ref enum_cast "cast<enum<EnumName>>()", which checks at run time
    that the value is a member of the enum.  See @ref enum_type_safety "Enum Type Safety".

    @section enum_syntax Enum Declaration Syntax
    <tt>[<b>public</b>]</tt> <tt><b>enum</b></tt> <em>enum_identifier</em> [<tt><b>:</b></tt> <em>base_type</em>] <tt>{</tt> \n
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<em>member_name</em> <tt>=</tt> <em>value</em><tt>,</tt> \n
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[...] \n
    <tt>}</tt>

    The base type specifier (<tt>: <em>base_type</em></tt>) is optional. When omitted, the base type
    is inferred from the first member value. If no members have explicit values, the base type
    defaults to @ref int_type "int". All member values must be of the same type as the enum's
    base type (whether explicit or inferred).

    @par Supported Base Types
    - @ref int_type "int" - integer values (default when no base type is specified and no values are given)
    - @ref string_type "string" - string values
    - @ref float_type "float" - floating-point values
    - @ref number_type "number" - arbitrary-precision numeric values

    @par Example: Integer Enum with Explicit Base Type
    @code{.py}
public enum Priority : int {
    Low = 0,
    Medium = 5,
    High = 10,
    Critical = 100,
}

# Use enum constants
Priority p = Priority::High;
printf("Priority value: %d\n", p);  # Prints: Priority value: 10
    @endcode

    @par Example: String Enum with Explicit Base Type
    @code{.py}
public enum Status : string {
    Active = "active",
    Inactive = "inactive",
    Pending = "pending",
}

# Use enum constants
Status s = Status::Active;
printf("Status: %s\n", s);  # Prints: Status: active
    @endcode

    @par Example: Inferred Base Type
    When the base type is omitted, it is inferred from the type of the first member value:
    @code{.py}
# Base type inferred as "string" from the first member value
enum HttpMethod {
    Get = "GET",
    Post = "POST",
    Put = "PUT",
    Delete = "DELETE",
}

# Base type inferred as "int" from the first member value
enum ExitCode {
    Success = 0,
    GeneralError = 1,
    MisuseOfBuiltin = 2,
}
    @endcode

    @par Example: Member Type Mismatch (Parse Error)
    All member values must match the enum's base type:
    @code{.py}
# PARSE-EXCEPTION: enum member 'B' value type 'int' does not match enum base type 'string'
enum Bad {
    A = "hello",
    B = 42,
}
    @endcode

    @section enum_type_safety Enum Type Safety
    Conversions between an enum and its base type are implicit in one direction only:

    |!Conversion|!Result
    |an enum value to its base type, or to a type that the base type converts to (ex: <tt>int i = Color::Red;</tt>)|allowed; the value takes the target type (here \c int), also when it is passed to a parameter of the base type or returned from code declared to return the base type
    |an enum value to a variable without a type, or of type \c auto|allowed; the value keeps its enum type
    |a value of the base type to an enum-typed variable or parameter (ex: <tt>Color c = 2;</tt>)|not allowed, even when the value equals the value of a member: a \c PARSE-TYPE-ERROR exception is raised at parse time when the parser knows the value's type; otherwise (ex: an \c auto variable) a \c RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR exception is raised at run time by an assignment, and a \c RUNTIME-OVERLOAD-ERROR exception by a call
    |a value of another enum type to an enum-typed variable or parameter|not allowed, with the same errors, even when both enums have the same base type
    |a value of the base type with @ref enum_cast "cast<enum<EnumName>>()"|allowed when the value is a member of the enum; otherwise a \c RUNTIME-CAST-ERROR exception is raised at run time

    An or-nothing enum type (ex: <tt>*Color</tt>) accepts \c NOTHING, so a parse-time check of an or-nothing
    base type value (ex: <tt>*int</tt>) assigned to it passes; a value other than \c NOTHING still raises
    \c RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR at run time.

    An enum value is equal to its base type value with the @ref logical_equality_operator "== operator", but not
    with the @ref logical_absolute_equality_operator "=== operator", as the types differ.

    @par Example: Type Compatibility
    @code{.py}
public enum Color : int {
    Red = 1,
    Green = 2,
    Blue = 3,
}

# Enum to base type - OK
int i = Color::Red;  # i = 1 (an int)

# Base type to enum - not allowed
int rawValue = 2;
Color c = rawValue;  # PARSE-TYPE-ERROR: lvalue for assignment operator '=' expects enum<Color>, but right-hand side is type 'int'

# Base type to enum with a cast - OK
Color c2 = cast<enum<Color>>(rawValue);  # c2 = Color::Green

# A base type value whose type is only known at run time
auto x = 3;
c2 = x;  # RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR: <lvalue> expects type 'enum<Color>', but got type 'integer' instead

# Function with enum parameter
sub processColor(Color c) {
    printf("Color value: %d\n", c);
}

processColor(Color::Blue);             # OK: enum constant
processColor(cast<enum<Color>>(3));    # OK: base type value converted with a cast
processColor(3);                       # PARSE-TYPE-ERROR: argument 'c' to 'processColor(enum<::Color> c)' expects enum<::Color>, but call supplies int
    @endcode

    @section enum_cast Casting to Enum Types
    The @ref cast "cast<>" operator converts a base type value to an enum value; it is the only way to do so.
    The value must have the enum's base type, or be the value of an enum with that base type; it is not
    converted to the base type:
    - a value that is a member of the enum is returned as that enum value
    - a value of the base type that is not a member of the enum raises a \c RUNTIME-CAST-ERROR exception at
      run time, also when the value is a literal
    - a value of another type raises a \c PARSE-EXCEPTION at parse time when the parser knows its type (ex:
      <tt>cast&lt;enum&lt;Color&gt;&gt;(2.0)</tt>), and otherwise a \c RUNTIME-CAST-ERROR exception at run time
    - \c NOTHING raises a \c PARSE-EXCEPTION at parse time when the parser knows that the value is \c NOTHING or
      cannot be the base type (ex: a literal \c NOTHING, or a <tt>*string</tt> value cast to an \c int enum), and
      otherwise (ex: a <tt>*int</tt> value cast to an \c int enum) a \c RUNTIME-CAST-ERROR exception at run time;
      the or-nothing form <tt>cast&lt;*enum&lt;EnumName&gt;&gt;()</tt> accepts \c NOTHING and returns it

    A value of another enum type with the same base type is treated like its base type value: it is converted
    when its value is a member of the target enum.

    @par Syntax
    <tt><b>cast</b>&lt;<b>enum</b>&lt;<em>EnumName</em>&gt;&gt;(</tt><em>expression</em><tt>)</tt> \n
    <tt><b>cast</b>&lt;<b>*enum</b>&lt;<em>EnumName</em>&gt;&gt;(</tt><em>expression</em><tt>)</tt> - allows NOTHING

    @par Example: Valid Cast
    @code{.py}
public enum Status : string {
    Active = "active",
    Inactive = "inactive",
}

string s = "active";
Status st = cast<enum<Status>>(s);  # OK: "active" is a valid Status value
    @endcode

    @par Example: Invalid Cast (Runtime Error)
    @code{.py}
string s = "unknown";
Status st = cast<enum<Status>>(s);  # RUNTIME-CAST-ERROR: "unknown" is not a valid Status value
    @endcode

    @par Example: Wrong Base Type (Runtime Error)
    @code{.py}
auto v = 123;
Status st = cast<enum<Status>>(v);  # RUNTIME-CAST-ERROR: cannot cast from type 'integer' to enum 'Status'; expected string value
    @endcode

    @par Example: Or-Nothing Cast
    @code{.py}
*string s;  # NOTHING
*Status st = cast<*enum<Status>>(s);  # OK: st is NOTHING

*string s2 = "active";
*Status st2 = cast<*enum<Status>>(s2);  # OK: st2 is Status::Active
    @endcode

    @section enum_lvalue_modification Modifying Enum Lvalues
    An lvalue declared with an enum type (a variable, a parameter, an object or hashdecl member, or the element of a
    list or hash whose element type is an enum) holds only the members of its enum.  An operator that changes such an
    lvalue in place computes a plain value of the enum's base type, which the enum type refuses as it refuses its
    assignment: the operator raises a \c RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR exception and leaves the lvalue unchanged, also if the
    result is the value of a member.  This applies to the compound assignment operators (@ref plus_equals_operator
    "+=" and the others), @ref pre_increment_operator "++" and @ref pre_decrement_operator "--",
    @ref splice "splice", @ref extract "extract", @ref trim "trim", @ref chomp "chomp", the @ref remove "remove" and
    @ref delete "delete" of a list or a range of characters of a string (of a single character, which they only
    read, they raise no error), the @ref regex_subst_operator "regular expression substitution"
    operator and the @ref transliteration_operator "transliteration" operator; @ref trim "trim" and @ref chomp "chomp"
    of a list or hash whose elements have a string enum type raise the error before changing any element.  An enum
    lvalue with no value is left without one.

    An lvalue with no type (or of type \c auto) that holds an enum value is changed as an lvalue holding the base
    value is: it then holds a plain value of the base type.  The value of the enum member itself never changes.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
enum Suffix : string {
    Long = "long",
}

Suffix s = Suffix::Long;
try {
    s += "er";
} catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
    # RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR: <+= operator> expects type 'enum<Suffix>', but got type 'string' instead
    printf("%s: %s\n", ex.err, ex.desc);
}
# s is still Suffix::Long

auto a = Suffix::Long;
a += "er";  # a is the plain string "longer"; Suffix::Long is still "long"
    @endcode

    @section enum_namespaces Namespaced Enums
    Enums can be declared within namespaces and accessed using the namespace path.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
namespace MyApp {
    public enum LogLevel : int {
        Debug = 0,
        Info = 1,
        Warning = 2,
        Error = 3,
    }
}

# Access namespaced enum
MyApp::LogLevel level = MyApp::LogLevel::Warning;
    @endcode

    @section enum_switch Using Enums in Switch Statements
    Enum constants can be used in switch statements for pattern matching.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
public enum Status : string {
    Active = "active",
    Inactive = "inactive",
    Pending = "pending",
}

sub getStatusMessage(Status s) returns string {
    switch (s) {
        case Status::Active:
            return "The item is active";
        case Status::Inactive:
            return "The item is inactive";
        case Status::Pending:
            return "The item is pending";
    }
}
    @endcode

    @section enum_reflection Enum Reflection
    Enums can be inspected at runtime using the reflection module.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
%requires reflection

public enum Priority : int {
    Low = 1,
    Medium = 2,
    High = 3,
}

# Get enum by name
Enum e = Enum::forName("Priority");
printf("Enum name: %s\n", e.getName());
printf("Base type: %s\n", e.getBaseType().getName());

# Iterate over members
foreach EnumMember m in (e.getMembers()) {
    printf("  %s = %y\n", m.getName(), m.getValue());
}

# Validate values
printf("Is 2 valid? %y\n", e.isValidValue(2));   # True
printf("Is 99 valid? %y\n", e.isValidValue(99)); # False
    @endcode

    @section enum_type_name Direct Enum Type Names
    Enum names can be used directly as type names without the <tt>enum&lt;...&gt;</tt> wrapper.
    Both syntaxes are equivalent.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
public enum Status : string {
    Active = "active",
}

# These are equivalent:
Status s1 = Status::Active;
enum<Status> s2 = Status::Active;

# Or-nothing variants:
*Status s3;
*enum<Status> s4;

# In function parameters:
sub process(Status s) { }
sub process2(enum<Status> s) { }
    @endcode

    @section enum_restrictions Parse Option Restrictions
    Enum declarations can be disabled using the @ref Qore::PO_NO_ENUM "PO_NO_ENUM" parse option or the
    @ref no-enum "%no-enum" parse directive.

    @code{.py}
%no-enum

# The following will cause a parse error:
# public enum Status : string { Active = "active" }
    @endcode

    @see
    - @ref cast "cast<>"
    - \c Qore::Reflection::Enum "Enum class"
    - \c Qore::Reflection::EnumMember "EnumMember class"
*/
